# goal The config is the only place a deployment is described

Everything that varies between installs lives in one YAML file and is fully
validated before the server binds a port. A misconfiguration must fail at startup
with a message naming the file, the path and the value — never at request time,
where it looks like a broken agent.

# scope What this module owns

In: reading the file, `${VAR}` expansion, validation, defaults, path resolution,
adapter resolution, MCP normalization.

Out: everything else. This module performs no network access, spawns nothing, and
knows nothing about HTTP or ACP message shapes. `loadConfig` is the only function
here that touches the filesystem.

# invariant Validation is total {#total_validation}

Every field that reaches the rest of the program has been checked here. Downstream
code may assume `config.server.port` is an integer in range, `agents[].command` is a
non-empty string, `limitPatterns` are compiled `RegExp`s, and `mcpServers` are in
ACP's exact wire shape. No defensive re-checking anywhere else — if a value can be
wrong, it is rejected here.

Each structured level has one declarative schema: valid keys, type, coercion,
default and enum values live together. Unknown keys are rejected from those schema
keys with an edit-distance suggestion; only explicitly free-form mappings (`env`,
`options`, labels, MCP headers/env) stop recursion. **A new option is added to its
schema or it does not exist** — never add a parallel default, key list, or coercion
branch that a future field can miss. Its commit also needs a test for acceptance and
rejection.

# func normalizeConfig {#normalize}

The single entry point for turning parsed YAML into the validated shape. `loadConfig`
is a thin wrapper that adds file reading and error prefixing.

## arg raw
The parsed YAML object. Rejected unless it is a mapping.

## arg options.baseDir
Directory relative paths resolve against — the config file's own directory, so a
config is portable between a checkout and a container without absolute paths.
Defaults to `process.cwd()`.

## arg options.env
The environment used for `${VAR}` expansion. Injected rather than read from
`process.env` directly, so tests are hermetic.

## result
`{server, agents}` — never a partial object. Throws `ConfigError` instead.

## error ConfigError
Every validation failure. `loadConfig` re-wraps it with the file path prefixed.
Never throw a bare `Error` from this module: the CLI prints `ConfigError` as a clean
message and anything else as a stack trace.

# rule ${VAR} expansion happens before validation, and yields strings {#env_strings}

`${VAR}` and `${VAR:-fallback}` expand recursively over the whole document first, so
validation sees final values. A set-but-empty bare `${VAR}` stays empty; `:-` uses
its fallback for either unset or empty, matching its shell meaning. An unset bare
variable is **fatal** — the alternative is a server that silently starts with a
half-expanded config. `$${VAR}` emits literal `${VAR}` text, which is required for
free text such as warm-up prompts and MCP headers.

Because expansion produces strings, every integer/count/`*Ms` field uses the
schema's integer coercion, every boolean uses its boolean coercion, and
`maxContextFill` uses the float coercion. Coercion is deliberately narrow, so
nonsense still fails validation rather than becoming `NaN` or truthy by accident.

# rule Listener addresses are normalized here

`server.port` is an integer from 1 through 65535. `metricsAddr` is either `"off"`
or normalized once into `{host, port}`; consumers must not split the source string
again. Hostnames/IPv4 use `host:port`; IPv6 is supported only in unambiguous
bracketed form, `[::1]:9090`.

# rule CORS is an explicit exposure decision

`server.cors` defaults to `false`. A non-empty origin string permits that origin;
`true` deliberately selects `*`. The server has no authentication, so browser
access must never become default-open. Environment-expanded boolean strings use
the same coercion as the other boolean fields.

# func resolvePreset {#resolve_preset}

See [_.hint#global_install](../_.hint) for why the bare bin name is never spawned.

## result
`{command, args}` — either `{process.execPath, [<adapter entry>]}` when the adapter
resolves, or `{<bin name>, []}` as the fallback.

## invariant Resolution never throws
An absent optional adapter must not stop the server: agents of other types still
work, and the missing one fails per-request as a clean 503 naming the command.

# rule An explicit command bypasses preset resolution entirely {#explicit_command}

`command` in the config wins and brings `args: []` with it, not the preset's args.
Merging the two would silently append adapter flags to someone's custom binary.

# test config

- defaults fill in, and `cwd` resolves against `baseDir`
- an absolute `cwd` is left alone; a per-agent `cwd` resolves against the server one
- `claude`/`codex` resolve to the installed adapter entry, `general` demands `command`
- an explicit `command` overrides the preset and does not inherit its args
- `resolvePreset` falls back to the bare bin name when the package is absent
- duplicate agent names are rejected — they are the OpenAI model ids
- unknown keys at every structured level name their path and closest valid key
- `port`, `permission`, `unsupportedParams`, `type`, `name`, `args` each rejected when malformed
- `${VAR}` preserves an empty value, `${VAR:-default}` falls back for empty/unset,
  `$${VAR}` stays literal, and an unset bare `${VAR}` throws
- every numeric and boolean field accepts its env-expanded string form, but nonsense still fails
- `metricsAddr` parses hostname/IPv4 and bracketed IPv6, and rejects malformed or out-of-range ports
- `cors` defaults off, accepts one origin or an explicit wildcard, and rejects malformed values
- `limitPatterns` compile case-insensitively and replace the default list when given
- an invalid `limitPatterns` regex is a path-qualified `ConfigError`, not a raw `SyntaxError`
- MCP mappings convert to `[{name, value}]`; stdio gets `args`/`env` even when omitted
- `continuity` accepts a boolean, and a "false"/"0" string from ${VAR}
- `loadConfig` prefixes errors with the file path and reports an unreadable file
